     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order because the dose looks unsafe.\n\nMetformin 1700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg/day. The maximum labeled dose for immediate-release metformin is 2,550 mg/day (commonly 2,000\u20132,500 mg/day in practice), so this is about twice the ceiling. A single 1700 mg dose also isn't a standard strength; tablets are typically 500, 850, or 1000 mg.\n\nIt may be a typo. Some possibilities:\n- 500 mg TID (1,500 mg/day)\n- 850 mg TID (2,550 mg/day, the labeled maximum)\n- 1000 mg BID (2,000 mg/day)\n\nPlease confirm the intended dose, frequency, and formulation (immediate- or extended-release), and I'll enter the corrected order. If 1700 mg TID is truly intended, please confirm that and tell me the clinical reason, and I can enter it with a note. Also check the patient's renal function. Metformin is generally avoided when eGFR is below 30 and dose-reduced at 30\u201345, and you've mentioned normal kidney function, so that part is fine."
      }
     ],
